WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.299
LG: I am Larry Goldstein. I am a professor of cellular and molecular medicine

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.532
at UC San Diego, and I have the great pleasure today to interview

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:30.799
my friend and colleague, comrade in arms, Paul Berg, the Nobel Prize winner

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.532
and also a recipient of the public policy award from the

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.832
American Society  for Cell Biology. I am going to ask Paul some questions

00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:40.932
about his life as a science policy advocate

00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:46.066
and public figure.  So, Paul, thank you for taking the time to join me today.

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.732
PB: Thank you.

00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.532
LG: Good. So, let's start at the beginning.

00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:55.699
You had a very illustrious career in science,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:00.599
but something made you decide to leave the lab at least part of the time

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.366
and engage in policy debates. What was it that brought you out for the first time?

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:11.032
PB: Well, it certainly wasn't voluntary. It turned out

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:15.466
that an experiment which we had planned and were in the midst of

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.932
elicited a lot of concern among other scientists.

00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:23.332
The experiment we had in progress was to fuse a small

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:29.932
minichromosome containing lambda genes and three genes of E. coli, the GAL operon,

00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:34.366
and we fused that with SV40 to make a recombinant that it contained.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:40.532
Now this recombinant could propagate in E. coli because it contained the parts on the mini plasmid

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.766
which allowed it to propagate in E. coli.

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:47.166
and it could also be propagated and transfect mammalian cells.

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:48.666
So it was kind of a dual-headed thing,

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:51.766
and what we wanted to was to be able to make mutations in

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:55.932
the SV40 genome in E. coli and then test them in mammalian cells.

00:01:55.933 --> 00:02:00.666
And the other thing is that we would want to test whether the GAL genes could be expressed

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.166
in mammalian cells. So that was the idea of the experiment.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:09.299
The point was that we were going to be propagating SV40 genes in E. coli.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:15.466
So the concern was that E. coli is a natural inhabitant in humans

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:20.966
and the concern was inadvertently, could you infect humans

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:25.432
with the E. coli you were propagating and could that possibly lead to cancer.

00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:32.166
That was the concern. And depending upon where you came down on the topic

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:38.066
you were either vociferous about it or you were just saying, "Forget about it. It's not serious."

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:44.032
Ok, but soon after a lot of conversations I became convinced that

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:48.032
I could not dismiss the risk, make it zero likelihood.

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:52.832
So I thought, well we'll just put it on the shelf and the whole thing died down.

00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:59.832
A year later, well, not quite a year later, Janet Mertz, a graduate student in my lab,

00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:02.466
discovered that EcoRI made cohesive ends

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:06.299
and she discovered that she could take any two DNAs and join them together.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.932
And so Stan Cohen and Herb Boyer then began to actually

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:13.866
make a lot of recombinants using E. coli plasmids

00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:19.899
and checking drug resistance and showing that they could make chimeric DNA molecules.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.266
And they talked about it at a Gordon conference,

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:29.099
and that elicited the same kind of response amongst a small minority of the audience

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.432
who said, "Oh my god, you guys can really make some dangerous things."

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:37.699
So what happened is that at that Gordon conference they got Maxine Singer

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.499
and Dieter Soll, who were the co-chairs,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.932
to draft a letter to the academy and to publish that letter in Science,

00:03:45.933 --> 00:03:50.166
calling attention to the great promise that being able to make new

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:56.432
kinds of DNA molecules, but also calling attention to the possible dangers and risks.

00:03:56.433 --> 00:04:01.399
And when the president of the academy got this letter

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.132
he said, "What do I do with it?"

00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:08.532
and Maxine said to him, "Call Paul Berg, because he's thought about this problem,"

00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:14.766
and so he asked me would I put together a group of people who could advise the academy

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:17.766
on how to respond to the concerns that had been raised.

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:25.932
And so I did. I put together a small group, and we met at MIT.

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:32.999
Dave Baltimore, Dan Nathans, myself, Jim Watson, a few other people, Norton Zinder,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.766
people who would understand the nature of the problem.

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:42.566
We met for a whole day this group, and in the end there was no advice that we could provide.

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:47.166
We didn't know what to do. So we said, you know, literally you could pick up the phone

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:51.299
and call everybody of your scientific community who is likely to use the technology

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.766
and say, "Hey, be careful." But we couldn't do that.

00:04:55.767 --> 00:05:00.766
So we said the next best thing was why don't we just publish a letter in Science,

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:08.766
calling attention to the promise, to some of the potential risks that have been raised and

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:19.799
essentially asking the scientific community to put a short hold on three kinds of experiments.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.966
We didn't call for a moratorium on all this kind of work.

00:05:22.967 --> 00:05:25.999
There were three kinds of experiments that we specifically called attention to.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.866
And in the letter we proposed having an international conference to

00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:32.032
six or eight months later

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:36.366
to examine the risks in more detail and come forward with some recommendations.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:42.132
I think the scientific community got a lot of good kudos from the public for saying

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:45.199
here is the problem, scientists called attention to it.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:45.966
LG: You actually got out in front of it.

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:48.799
PB: as proposing how to deal with this thing.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.832
So if you look back now, you say I got into public policy

00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:58.532
through my own experiments and just being called on

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:02.999
to say what do we do when somebody raised the risks.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.432
So the initial so called moratorium letter that came out of the group

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:14.032
called for a meeting to deal with it, and so we convened

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:16.899
I was the chair of convening the Asilomar meeting

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.599
and the organizing committee was Maxine, Dave Baltimore,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.866
myself, Zinder, Sydney Brenner, we had another person from Europe,

00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:28.199
and we were going to organize a meeting of experts

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.132
to try to assess what was the magnitude of the risk,

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:33.566
what was the likelihood of them coming true,

00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:36.999
and how could we proceed in any case.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:43.466
Ok, so the Asilomar meeting was a major event. A hundred and fifty people,

00:06:43.467 --> 00:06:48.966
a huge representation from the media, which was probably one of the best things we decided.

00:06:48.967 --> 00:06:53.399
There were fifteen people from the press and newspapers, magazines, and so on.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:58.499
And in those three days we debated.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.932
What are the kinds of experiments we want to do?

00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:02.866
What are the possible risks?

00:07:02.867 --> 00:07:05.766
And we organized it very well so that the meeting was not all talking about

00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:08.399
risks, it was talking about science.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.399
and that was probably the saving grace of why the meeting succeeded

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.399
because people came prepared to talk about scientific experiments

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.999
and they were balanced by discussion.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.499
There was a lot of interesting science that came out of the Asilomar meeting.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.866
One was the safe bug-the engineered microbe which could not escape from the lab.

00:07:27.867 --> 00:07:29.799
LG: Right, so the crippled hosts.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.699
PB: The crippled hosts. And the second one, so that was Sydney Brenner's idea.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:35.799
LG: It's a good idea.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:39.899
PB: He brought that to the meeting and I think that

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.332
was what turned people around a lot

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:47.532
because suddenly we could now answer that question that we could devise

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:53.632
the experiments in some way that would markedly lessen the likelihood of escape.

00:07:53.633 --> 00:07:57.966
So this organism wouldn't be able to live outside the lab.

00:07:57.967 --> 00:08:02.666
So there were a number...so right in that meeting

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:07.999
you could see science taking over, people thinking oh we can do this,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.499
LG: And I think you and I would agree, if you are going to have good science policy,

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.799
it has to be driven by good science.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.266
Misunderstanding is not going to get the job done.

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:19.466
PB: The turning point of the meeting

00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:25.699
frankly was the last night when the lawyers had their session

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.166
and the ethicists.  We'd invited a group of lawyers and ethicists.

00:08:29.167 --> 00:08:31.866
LG: And did the lawyers and ethicists sit through the science as well?

00:08:31.867 --> 00:08:34.432
PB: They participated in everything, as did the press.

00:08:34.433 --> 00:08:36.899
The press were free to ask questions, raise points,

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:45.632
and in the drinking in the evenings, they milled around and were full members of the meeting.

00:08:45.633 --> 00:08:53.832
The lawyers pointed out the legal problems, and challenged Jim Watson

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:59.799
with the idea, because he had now turned, and he was totally opposed to any kind of regulation,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:03.099
whereas he had been a signer of the original letter.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.399
They said people could close down Cold Spring Harbor

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.032
if there is any danger or anything that happens.

00:09:11.033 --> 00:09:14.799
And then people's own personal risks were

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.899
beginning to be played on and eventually I think everybody

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.199
said we cannot walk out of here tomorrow morning

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:27.566
saying we don't have any advice.  So that night...

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:28.399
LG: Well, especially with the press there.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.166
PB: Exactly.

00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:30.066
LG: Because they were going to cover it, right?

00:09:30.067 --> 00:09:36.699
PB: And that night, I and Maxine, and Dave and Sydney, we drafted a set of guidelines,

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:43.599
and recommended that the NIH use this as a sort of outline

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.899
for a set of guidelines that would essentially determine the conditions

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.999
under which this research could be done, and the conditions under which it should not be done.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.332
And so the whole thing that came out was very positive.

00:09:56.333 --> 00:09:59.266
We debated it, and voted on it segment by segment

00:09:59.267 --> 00:10:03.766
until when noon came when we had to leave.

00:10:03.767 --> 00:10:07.232
We had an approved list of regulations.

00:10:07.233 --> 00:10:08.332
LG: Was it unanimous or was consensus?

00:10:08.333 --> 00:10:13.666
PB: No, no, there were... I would say there was 95% in favor of going forward,

00:10:13.667 --> 00:10:17.332
even though the language was muddled and things like that,

00:10:17.333 --> 00:10:20.632
] but we all agreed that we would smooth things out.

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:24.566
So I worked on the language, and Dave did, and Maxine.

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:28.532
And we finally got a document, which is now recorded

00:10:28.533 --> 00:10:31.532
as a famous document out of the Asilomar conference.

00:10:31.533 --> 00:10:34.732
We look back on it and say, well you know,

00:10:34.733 --> 00:10:38.166
a bunch of amateurs, we mumbled through a bunch of stuff.

00:10:38.167 --> 00:10:41.932
We made some decisions that were smart in retrospect.

00:10:41.933 --> 00:10:46.466
For example, one of the things we did not do and did not include in any way

00:10:46.467 --> 00:10:51.466
was the ethics. We didn't talk about genetic testing.

00:10:51.467 --> 00:10:52.599
We didn't talk about any of that.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.066
We talked about real experiments.

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:58.466
And what the impact of those experiments would be in the field.

00:10:58.467 --> 00:11:02.966
So there were agriculture, nitrogen fixation, people had all kinds of ideas

00:11:02.967 --> 00:11:05.132
of what we call the low hanging fruit.

00:11:05.133 --> 00:11:06.866
LG: The infamous low hanging fruit.

00:11:06.867 --> 00:11:10.032
PB: What were the things we were going to do right away? Clone insulin gene, clone the growth hormone gene.

00:11:10.033 --> 00:11:11.866
All of those things were obvious.

00:11:11.867 --> 00:11:15.032
In retrospect, when you look back, you say

00:11:15.033 --> 00:11:19.432
most of what has happened, no one could even conceive of it at the time.

00:11:19.433 --> 00:11:25.032
And so that's the way science goes. You make a slight

00:11:25.033 --> 00:11:27.499
breakthrough, you've got a lot of very smart people out there.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.932
Very smart people figure out very quickly how to use

00:11:30.933 --> 00:11:34.166
this new notion, this new tool, or whatever

00:11:34.167 --> 00:11:37.766
and the field explodes, and that is what happened

00:11:37.767 --> 00:11:40.832
and why recombinant DNA has had the impact that it has.

00:11:40.833 --> 00:11:43.199
LG: So when did the Congress get into the act?

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.832
When did the politicians decide that this was something they should worry about?

00:11:46.833 --> 00:11:53.999
PB: Okay, so, the Asilomar meeting was held in February of 1975.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.899
The rest of that year there were small groups that were meeting to

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:02.232
devise the guidelines.  The guidelines came out

00:12:02.233 --> 00:12:08.932
in the summer of '76. And that ruled the day.

00:12:08.933 --> 00:12:15.332
And then sniping occurred. And the sniping occurred from different people

00:12:15.333 --> 00:12:19.432
who said, "well, these guidelines are not rigorous enough,"

00:12:19.433 --> 00:12:22.466
or, "we don't trust the scientists or abide by them. "

00:12:22.467 --> 00:12:26.799
Congress began to respond to these little barbs

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.899
and they set on a course that they were

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:33.166
actually going to prohibit recombinant DNA research in the country.

00:12:33.167 --> 00:12:35.799
And I remember spending a lot of time in Congress

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:39.566
and I can remember vividly one Senator

00:12:39.567 --> 00:12:42.032
from Arkansas who is normally a liberal

00:12:42.033 --> 00:12:45.866
and was a pretty good guy getting up and saying on the floor of the Senate,

00:12:45.867 --> 00:12:49.532
"I never got through high school chemistry,

00:12:49.533 --> 00:12:53.132
and I don't profess to understand any of this science,

00:12:53.133 --> 00:12:57.666
but I believe this is the most dangerous work ever undertaken in this country,

00:12:57.667 --> 00:13:01.932
and I would recommend prohibiting it."

00:13:01.933 --> 00:13:04.066
There were a lot of people who shared that view,

00:13:04.067 --> 00:13:09.399
and I am cynical enough to now realize that what changed the whole picture

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:15.032
and obliterated the concerns was the founding of Genentech

00:13:15.033 --> 00:13:17.832
and the first phase stock issue.

00:13:17.833 --> 00:13:19.432
LG: Ahh, money.

00:13:19.433 --> 00:13:22.932
PB: Money. Suddenly it became clear that this was going to be

00:13:22.933 --> 00:13:25.732
a commercial entity and important,

00:13:25.733 --> 00:13:29.166
and to put any prohibitions on it was like shooting yourself in the foot.

00:13:29.167 --> 00:13:30.999
And the whole thing disappeared.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:35.266
LG: So the promise of making insulin for kids was not the winning message, you think?

00:13:35.267 --> 00:13:38.832
PB: It was a message, but when you actually saw something happen

00:13:38.833 --> 00:13:44.466
that was the, I believe, that was thing that actually was the switch.

00:13:44.467 --> 00:13:48.332
And the atmosphere changed completely as soon as

00:13:48.333 --> 00:13:50.599
it became a couple of companies starting up

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:52.799
to take advantage of this technology

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.932
to do this that and the other thing.

00:13:54.933 --> 00:13:58.566
LG: So ultimately the resolution of this was, if I understand,

00:13:58.567 --> 00:14:02.532
if I remember correctly, no law was ever passed in the Congress,

00:14:02.533 --> 00:14:04.132
PB: No law was passed.

00:14:04.133 --> 00:14:09.566
LG: And it was all done by scientists driving the development of good regulation

00:14:09.567 --> 00:14:11.532
through the funding bodies, which then spread.

00:14:11.533 --> 00:14:12.766
PB: Exactly.

00:14:12.767 --> 00:14:14.932
LG: Amazing. That's a fantastic example.

00:14:14.933 --> 00:14:16.733
PB: Yeah.

